==English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|inhability||disqualification for office}}, equivalent to {{af|en|in-|ability}}. Compare {{cog|frm|inhabilité}}, {{cog|ML.|inhabilitās}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪnəˈbɪlɪti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-inability.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{U|lack}} of the [[ability]] to do something; [[incapability]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Shaw|chapter=Of Religion|title=The Fundamental Doctrines of the Church of England,{{nb...|As ſet down in Her Catechism, Explain'd, and Vindicated from the Calumnies of the Romaniſts, and the Censures of the Pꝛesbyteꝛians and Other Dissenters}}|volume=I|year=1720|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by H. P. for}} George Strahan,{{nb...|at the Golden Ball in Cornhill; and}} William Mears,{{nb...|at the Lamb without Temple-Bar.}}|page=36|pageurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/The_Fundamental_Doctrines_of_the_Church/cWLG9hfkuTcC?gbpv=1&pg=PA36|passage={{...}} tho' theſe ſeem'd to be very unfit Inſtruments for compaſſing of that great Deſign for which they were then employ'd, becauſe of their '''Inability''' and Uncapacity in performing the Work ſo very great and important; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-11-26|author=Daniel Taylor|title=Jack Wilshere scores twice to ease Arsenal to victory over Marseille|journal=w:The Guardian|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/nov/26/arsenal-marseille-match-report-champions-league|text=The Premier League leaders did what many people thought was beyond them in their last European excursion, at the home of {{w|Borussia Dortmund}}, and they made light work of overcoming Marseille on a night when the one-sidedness was not reflected by their '''inability''' to add to {{w|Jack Wilshere}}'s two goals.}}
